# FLUIDD UI Setup

This guide explains how to back up your current Klipper configuration and install the supplied configuration files and macros.

## 1. Back up your existing configuration

In Fluidd, click the `Configuration` button on the left-hand side.

### Back up the `KAMP` folder

1. Right-click the `KAMP` folder.
2. Select `Create ZIP`.
3. Right-click the newly created ZIP file and download it to your PC.

### Back up `printer.cfg`

Right-click `printer.cfg` and download it to your PC.

### Back up `gcode_macro.cfg`

1. Open the `klipper-macros-qd` folder.
2. Right-click `gcode_macro.cfg`.
3. Download it to your PC.

You should now have copies of the original, unchanged files safely stored on your PC. Do not skip this step.

---

## 2. Rename the original files

Before installing the supplied files, rename the existing configuration files so they are preserved.

Rename the following:

- `KAMP.cfg` → `KAMP.cfg-original`
- `printer.cfg` → `printer.cfg-original`
- `klipper-macros-qd` → `klipper-macros-qd-original`

Then open the `KAMP` folder and rename:

- `adaptive_meshing.cfg` → `adaptive_meshing.cfg-original`
- `KAMP_Settings.cfg` → `KAMP_Settings.cfg-original`

These original files are retained as backups and can be restored if required.

---

## 3. Install the supplied KAMP files

Open the `KAMP` folder.

At the top of the page, click `+ ADD` and select `Upload Files`.

Upload the supplied files:

- `adaptive_Meshing.cfg`
- `KAMP_Settings.cfg`

Once uploaded, click the blue folder icon to return to the main configuration directory.

---

## 4. Install the main configuration files

Click `+ ADD` again and upload:

- `printer.cfg`
- `My-Configs.cfg`
- `gcode_macro.cfg`

Once all the files have been uploaded, double-click any configuration file to open it.

In the top-right corner, click:

`SAVE & RESTART`

This will save the changes and restart Klipper.

---

## 5. Organising macros in Fluidd

You can organise the available macros into groups so they are easier to find.

In Fluidd:

1. Click the `Settings` button on the left.
2. Scroll down to `Macros`.
3. All of the macros will appear in the `uncategorised` section.
4. Create and organise the useful macros into categories such as:
   - Calibration
   - Filament
   - Print

5. Click on a macro and choose the category you want to assign it to.
6. Only keep the macros you actually use and leave the rest in the uncategorised section. Aim for around 5–6 macros per category.
7. You can move them around to change the order in which they are displayed.

Some useful macros to look for include:

### Calibration
- `PID_BED`
- `PID_EXTRUDER`
- `PROBE_BED`
- `SCREWS_TILT`
- `Z_TILT`
- `START_LUBRICATION`
- `ALL_HEATERS_OFF`

### Filament
- `LOAD_FILAMENT`
- `UNLOAD_FILAMENT`

### Print
- `SET_PAUSE_AT_LAYER`
- `CANCEL_PRINT`
- `PAUSE`
- `RESUME`

Organising the macros this way gives you quick access to the functions you use most often.

